from html import escape
from pathlib import Path
sections=[
('Begin with purpose',[
('Identify what the interface needs to communicate','Start with the audience, website purpose, brand identity, user goals, and the intended emotional experience. Define the problem before choosing or changing colors. User goals should guide visual hierarchy.'),
('Turn intentions into a hypothesis','Color psychology can suggest possibilities, but context, audience, variations, typography, imagery, materials, and language all affect interpretation. Test the intended impression with people instead of assuming a color creates trust or a universal feeling.'),
('A starting workflow','1. Identify the purpose. 2. Define semantic roles such as primary action, warning, success, text, and background. 3. Choose base colors. 4. Evaluate accessibility, consistency, hierarchy, and usability. Content and voice decisions can inform the roles.')]),
('Build and distribute a palette',[
('Adjust the properties','Hue identifies the color family, saturation its intensity, and value its lightness or darkness. A vivid color is not necessarily light. Adjust the smallest relevant variable first and evaluate what changed.'),
('Develop variations','Tints, shades, tones, and color scales can extend a brand color without introducing unrelated hues. A palette can begin with a light neutral, dark neutral, primary, support, and accent; semantic colors and further variations can be added as needed. This is an example, not a required count.'),
('Assign responsibilities','Choose roles for text, backgrounds, actions, highlights, notices, destructive actions, and decoration. A brand color and an action color need not be the same. Two elements can share a color without sharing a role.'),
('Measure distribution in context','Coverage is the area a color occupies; frequency is how often it appears; prominence is how strongly it attracts attention. A small, repeated, high-contrast accent can dominate. Placement, surrounding colors, and proximity also affect attention.'),
('Use harmony deliberately','Color-wheel relationships can provide variety, but complementary colors do not guarantee prominence or accessible text contrast. In triadic or other varied palettes, distinct roles and deliberate distribution can help colors coexist.'),
('Consider breathing room','The amount of color, neutral space, photography, typography, and composition all contribute to whether an interface feels calm, welcoming, institutional, or overwhelming. Minimal does not require stark white. Warm neutrals can soften a design; gray can serve subtle surfaces or strong emphasis.')]),
('Accessibility and communication',[
('Start with accessibility','A pleasing color combination is not automatically readable or accessible. Check text contrast against applicable WCAG requirements, and check non-text contrast for relevant control boundaries, indicators, and states. A larger font can qualify for a different threshold but does not change the contrast ratio.'),
('Do not rely on color alone','Labels, explanatory text, icons, patterns, placement, or other cues can distinguish warning, success, information, and destructive actions. Cues must communicate the intended meaning in context. Screen-reader support and semantic markup also matter.'),
('Distinguish meaning from direction','An upward arrow and a numerical increase show direction, but whether that increase is desirable depends on what is being measured and the surrounding context. Color can reinforce that meaning.'),
('Make actions recognizable','An action’s label, icon, placement, shape, and context help people identify it. A destructive action can be recognizable without being the most visually dominant. Consider appropriate warnings, confirmation, undo, or recovery based on the consequences.'),
('Test states and focus','Review default, hover, focus, pressed, and disabled states as applicable. Hover supports pointer use and may not exist on touch devices. A focus indicator identifies the control receiving input; check its visibility, thickness, and contrast against adjacent colors, not only the page background. Applicable WCAG criteria and exceptions differ.')]),
('Tokens, components, and states',[
('Separate responsibilities','Primitive tokens provide values; semantic tokens describe purposes; component tokens can customize a component when needed. Reuse tokens where purposes genuinely match. More layers can make a system harder to maintain.'),
('Avoid unintended dependencies','A success color and selected navigation may look identical today while serving different roles. Separate names make it easier to evolve them independently. Changing a token can also change contrast, so review affected uses.'),
('Scope and inheritance','CSS custom properties can be defined globally or within a section. A scoped override changes the value in that context without changing the global value; avoid coupling a component’s identity to a particular color.'),
('Meaning, emphasis, and state','Component dimensions can be independent. For example, data-variant="destructive" can describe an action’s meaning while data-emphasis="primary" describes its visual importance. A destructive action can be primary inside a confirmation dialog. States such as hover and focus are separate concerns.'),
('Recognize the consequences','For deletion or other consequential actions, color alone is insufficient. Consider warning text, confirmation, recovery, placement, and accessible contrast. Data attributes are implementation choices, not substitutes for semantic HTML or accessible names.')]),
('Related design considerations',[
('Look beyond color','Spacing, placement, proximity, composition, typography, photography, shapes, materials, labels, and interaction all influence how a design feels and communicates. Elements placed close together can appear related; check whether they should be related.'),
('Investigate the complaint','If a design feels intimidating, examine typography, imagery, tone, spacing, and color distribution. If it is difficult to navigate, examine labels, information architecture, hierarchy, and accessibility. Do not assume changing the palette will fix the problem.'),
('Coordinate disciplines','Information architecture organizes destinations and information. UX design considers needs and consequences. Interaction design defines behavior. Visual hierarchy guides attention. Color supports all of them alongside content, accessibility, and graphic design.'),
('Consistency and expectations','Repeated visual treatments can create associations and expectations. Consistency means applying roles predictably, not making everything look identical. Similar colors used for different button meanings can cause confusion.')]),
('Questions and future work',[
('Research and measurement','How can a portfolio use user research, analytics, or A/B testing to evaluate whether a design communicates its intended feeling? How can attempted clicks be observed responsibly and interpreted against user goals?'),
('Design systems and style guides','How do a design system, UI library, frontend style guide, and design language relate? What should a reusable master style guide for the portfolio and repositories contain?'),
('Color and interface practice','Explore primary, secondary, and tertiary colors; accessible color-system reviews; color variants; interactive states; semantic token references; screen-reader support; and practical CSS examples.'),
('Tools and references','Compare color wheels with Adobe and review the separate color-theory Site for reusable ideas. Explore DaFont and 1001Fonts in the typography context. Review texture, metallic, vault, and glass effects using CSS, imagery, and graphic-design methods.'),
('Future collections','Build an action vocabulary across websites and an LLM-assisted workflow that connects design questions with colors, feelings, references, and other parameters. Consider a human-reviewed website analysis tool.'),
('Visual experiment','Explore a rounded dark shape at the top that grows darker on scroll, potentially with cyan, yellow, and magenta in three columns. This is a concept for later exploration.'),
('Separate project note','Nicotine Tracker: explore time between entries and possible chain-pouching patterns as a separate feature.')])]
nav='<header class="site-header"><div class="header-inner"><a class="brand" href="./"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">C</span><b>Color Learning</b></a><nav class="nav-links" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="./">Vocabulary</a><a href="notes.html" aria-current="page">Design notes</a><a href="./#associations">Color associations</a><a href="./#future">Future ideas</a></nav></div></header>'
body=[]
for title,items in sections:
 rows=''.join('<div class="vocab-entry"><dt>'+escape(t)+'</dt><dd><p>'+escape(d)+'</p></dd></div>' for t,d in items)
 body.append('<section class="vocab-group"><div class="vocab-group-heading"><h2>'+escape(title)+'</h2></div><dl>'+rows+'</dl></section>')
html='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Working notes on color and interface design."><title>Design notes | Color Learning</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="navigation.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="vocabulary.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="custom.css"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>'+nav+'<main id="main"><section class="intro"><h1>Color design notes</h1><div class="intro-bottom"><p>Working principles, examples, and open questions from the notes. We can refine and reorganize these as the site develops.</p></div></section>'+''.join(body)+'</main><footer><p>Color Learning · Notes in progress</p></footer></body></html>'
Path('dist/notes.html').write_text(html)
